To implement a Java program that computes the average age of people residing in the United Kingdom, follow these steps:
1. Create a Person class with an "age" attribute. Here's an example:
```
public class Person {
    private int age;
    public Person(int age) {
        this.age = age;
    }
    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }
}
```
2. Create a collection of Person objects that represent the UK population. You can use an ArrayList to store the Person objects. Here's an example:
```
List<Person> population = new ArrayList<>();
population.add(new Person(25));
population.add(new Person(35));
population.add(new Person(40));
population.add(new Person(30));
// Add more Person objects as needed
```
3. Iterate through the population list to calculate the total age. You can use a for loop to iterate through the list and add up the ages. Here's an example:
```
int totalAge = 0;
for (Person person : population) {
    totalAge += person.getAge();
}
```
4. Determine the average age of the population by dividing the total age by the size of the population list. Here's an example:
```
double averageAge = (double) totalAge / population.size();
```
5. Output the average age in a human-readable format. You can use System.out.println() to display the average age. Here's an example:
```
System.out.println("The average age of the UK population is " + averageAge + " years.");
```
Putting it all together, here's the complete Java program:
```
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Person> population = new ArrayList<>();
        population.add(new Person(25));
        population.add(new Person(35));
        population.add(new Person(40));
        population.add(new Person(30));
        int totalAge = 0;
        for (Person person : population) {
            totalAge += person.getAge();
        }
        double averageAge = (double) totalAge / population.size();
        System.out.println("The average age of the UK population is " + averageAge + " years.");
    }
}
class Person {
    private int age;
    public Person(int age) {
        this.age = age;
    }
    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }
}
```
When you run the program, it will output the following:
```
The average age of the UK population is 32.5 years.
```

Sure, here's an example of how you could create a program in C++ that generates a list of foods with high fiber content and low sugar:
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;
struct Food {
    string name;
    int fiber;
    int sugar;
};
bool compare(Food a, Food b) {
    if (a.fiber == b.fiber) {
        return a.sugar < b.sugar;
    }
    return a.fiber > b.fiber;
}
int main() {
    vector<Food> foods = {
        {"Avocado", 6, 1},
        {"Broccoli", 3, 1},
        {"Lentils", 8, 1},
        {"Artichoke", 7, 2},
        {"Peas", 5, 3},
        {"Sweet Potato", 4, 7},
        {"Banana", 2, 14},
        {"Grapes", 1, 16},
        {"Chocolate", 3, 50},
        {"Soda", 0, 40}
    };

    sort(foods.begin(), foods.end(), compare);

    for (Food food : foods) {
        if (food.fiber >= 5 && food.sugar <= 5) {
            cout << food.name << endl;
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
```
In this example, we define a `Food` struct that contains the name of the food, as well as its fiber and sugar content. We then create a vector of `Food` objects that represents our database of foods.
We define a `compare` function that we will use to sort the foods based on their fiber and sugar content. The function first compares the fiber content of the two foods. If they have the same fiber content, it compares their sugar content.
We then use the `sort` function to sort the foods in descending order of fiber content, and ascending order of sugar content.
Finally, we loop through the sorted list of foods and print out the names of the foods that have at least 5 grams of fiber and no more than 5 grams of sugar.
You can modify the `foods` vector to include your own database of foods and their nutritional information.

