In Java, there are two types of exceptions: checked and unchecked. Checked exceptions are those that are checked at compile time, and the programmer is required to handle them explicitly. Examples of checked exceptions include IOException and SQLException. Unchecked exceptions, on the other hand, are not checked at compile time and do not require explicit handling. Examples of unchecked exceptions include NullPointerException and ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.

