The most efficient algorithm to sort a large list of countries based on their GDP in C++ is the QuickSort algorithm. It has an average time complexity of O(nlogn) and is widely used in practice. Here's an example implementation in C++:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;
struct Country {
    string name;
    double gdp;
};
int partition(vector<Country>& countries, int low, int high) {
    double pivot = countries[high].gdp;
    int i = low - 1;
    for (int j = low; j < high; j++) {
        if (countries[j].gdp < pivot) {
            i++;
            swap(countries[i], countries[j]);
        }
    }
    swap(countries[i + 1], countries[high]);
    return i + 1;
}
void quickSort(vector<Country>& countries, int low, int high) {
    if (low < high) {
        int pi = partition(countries, low, high);
        quickSort(countries, low, pi - 1);
        quickSort(countries, pi + 1, high);
    }
}
int main() {
    vector<Country> countries = { {"USA", 21.44}, {"China", 14.14}, {"Japan", 5.15}, {"Germany", 4.17}, {"India", 2.87} };
    // Sort countries by GDP using QuickSort algorithm
    quickSort(countries, 0, countries.size() - 1);
    // Print sorted list of countries
    for (auto country : countries) {
        cout << country.name << " GDP: " << country.gdp << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
In this example, we define a `Country` struct with a `name` and `gdp` field. We then define the `partition` function to partition the list of countries based on their GDP, and the `quickSort` function to recursively sort the list using the partition function. Finally, we define a `main` function that creates a list of countries, sorts them using the QuickSort algorithm, and prints the sorted list.

