Yes, Java can be used to prevent user addiction to digital devices by implementing features such as limiting screen time, reminding users to take breaks from technology, and tracking user activity.
Here's an example of Java code that limits screen time and encourages taking breaks from technology:
```java
import java.util.Timer;
import java.util.TimerTask;
public class ScreenTimeReminder {
    private static final int MAX_SCREEN_TIME = 60; // in minutes
    private static final int BREAK_TIME = 10; // in minutes
    private static int screenTime = 0;
    private static Timer timer = new Timer();
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        startScreenTimeReminder();
    }
    private static void startScreenTimeReminder() {
        timer.schedule(new TimerTask() {
            @Override
            public void run() {
                screenTime++;
                if (screenTime >= MAX_SCREEN_TIME) {
                    System.out.println("You've reached your maximum screen time for the day. Please take a break.");
                    screenTime = 0;
                    startBreakReminder();
                }
            }
        }, 0, 60000); // 1 minute interval
    }
    private static void startBreakReminder() {
        timer.schedule(new TimerTask() {
            @Override
            public void run() {
                System.out.println("It's time to take a break from technology. Please step away from your device for " + BREAK_TIME + " minutes.");
                startScreenTimeReminder();
            }
        }, BREAK_TIME * 60000); // break time interval
    }
}
```
This code sets a maximum screen time of 60 minutes and reminds the user to take a break after reaching the limit. The break time is set to 10 minutes. The code uses a Timer to track the user's screen time and schedule reminders. 
