   b. Tokenize the text documents into individual words, using a tokenizer that does not store or transmit any sensitive data.
   c. Assign each document a unique identifier that does not contain any PII, such as a randomly generated UUID.
2. Conversion:
   a. Use a text-to-speech (TTS) engine to convert each tokenized document into an audio file.
   b. Store the audio files in a secure, encrypted database that is only accessible to authorized users.
3. Post-processing:
   a. Delete any temporary files or data generated during the conversion process.
   b. Perform regular security audits and vulnerability assessments to ensure the system remains HIPAA-compliant.
