What are the main differences between the K-Nearest Neighbours (KNN) and Linear Regression algorithms in machine learning? Can you explain their respective algorithmic structures and prediction performances in detail using the comparison chart below?
[Java code]
For KNN, the following Java code can be used to implement the algorithm:
import java.util.*;
public class KNN {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Get training data
        double[][] trainData = {{1.0, 2.0}, {2.0, 3.0}, {3.0, 4.0}, {4.0, 5.0}, {5.0, 6.0}};
        int[] trainLabels = {0, 0, 1, 1, 1};
        // Get testing data
        double[] testData = {2.5, 3.5};
        // Set number of neighbors to consider
        int k = 3;
        // Find k nearest neighbors
        PriorityQueue<double[]> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(k, (a, b) -> Double.compare(b[1], a[1]));
        for (int i = 0; i < trainData.length; i++) {
            double dist = euclideanDistance(trainData[i], testData);
            maxHeap.offer(new double[] {trainLabels[i], dist});
            if (maxHeap.size() > k) {
                maxHeap.poll();
            }
        }
        // Make prediction
        int[] freq = new int[2];
        while (!maxHeap.isEmpty()) {
            freq[(int)maxHeap.poll()[0]]++;
        }
        int prediction = freq[0] > freq[1] ? 0 : 1;
        System.out.println("Prediction: " + prediction);
    }
    public static double euclideanDistance(double[] x, double[] y) {
        double sum = 0.0;
        for (int i = 0; i < x.length; i++) {
            sum += Math.pow(x[i] - y[i], 2);
        }
        return Math.sqrt(sum);
    }
}
